<article class="env-lemma" id="0879">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0879">Lemma <span data-tag="0879">76.37.5</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $f : X \to Y$ be a morphism of algebraic spaces over $S$. Let $V \subset Y$ be an open subspace. If </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$f$ is separated, locally of finite type, and flat, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$f^{-1}(V) \to V$ is an isomorphism, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$V \to Y$ is quasi-compact and scheme theoretically dense, 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> then $f$ is an open immersion. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Applying Lemma <a href="/tag/0877" data-tag="0877">76.37.2</a> we see that $f$ is locally of finite presentation. Applying Lemma <a href="/tag/0878" data-tag="0878">76.37.3</a> we see that $f$ has relative dimension $\leq 0$. By Morphisms of Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/04NV" data-tag="04NV">67.34.6</a> this implies that $f$ is locally quasi-finite. By Morphisms of Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/0418" data-tag="0418">67.51.1</a> this implies that $f$ is representable. By Descent on Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/041X" data-tag="041X">74.11.14</a> we can check whether $f$ is an open immersion étale locally on $Y$. Hence we may assume that $Y$ is a scheme. Since $f$ is representable, we reduce to the case of schemes which is More on Flatness, Lemma <a href="/tag/081M" data-tag="081M">38.11.5</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>